/**

@page page_exahype_tracer Tracers

\tableofcontents

ExaHyPE tracers use Peano particle sets. A project declares the particle type,
inserts tracer coordinates, projects solver fields onto the particles, and can
write their histories to CSV files.

# Adding a tracer

The project creates a generated particle type:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
tracerParticles = project.add_tracer(name="MyTracer")
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

An insertion action set adds initial coordinates during grid initialisation:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
project.add_action_set_to_initialisation(
  exahype2.tracer.InsertParticlesByCoordinates(
    particle_set=tracerParticles,
    coordinates=[[x0, y0], [x1, y1]],
  )
)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The coordinate dimension matches the generated project. Other insertion action
sets create Cartesian seeds, randomised seeds, or read coordinates from a
file.

# Projecting solver data

A tracing action set samples the solution at each particle. A finite-volume
configuration can use:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
tracing = exahype2.tracer.FiniteVolumesTracing(
  tracerParticles,
  solver,
  project_on_tracer_properties_kernel=
    "::exahype2::fv::projectAllValuesOntoParticle_piecewiseLinear",
)
project.add_action_set_to_initialisation(tracing)
project.add_action_set_to_timestepping(tracing)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Initialisation populates tracer fields before the first plot or database
record. Time-stepping updates them with the evolving solution. DG solvers use
the projection routines under `exahype2/dg/Tracer.h`; finite-volume and
finite-difference solvers use `exahype2/fv/Tracer.h`.

# Writing trajectories

exahype2.tracer.DumpTracerIntoDatabase collects particle samples and writes
rank-local CSV snapshots below the runtime output path:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
project.add_action_set_to_timestepping(
  exahype2.tracer.DumpTracerIntoDatabase(
    particle_set=tracerParticles,
    solver=solver,
    filename="Tracer",
    position_delta_between_two_snapshots=1.0e16,
    time_delta_between_two_snapshots=0.01,
    output_precision=10,
  )
)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Each row contains the stable particle number, time stamp, coordinates, and
tracked values. The particle number combines the originating rank with a
rank-local counter, so insertion requires no global numbering collective. A
particle keeps this identity after it migrates.

An MPI run appends each rank's records to `<filename>-rank-<rank>.csv`; a
serial run writes `<filename>.csv`. Postprocessing combines rank files by
particle number and time stamp.

# Moving tracers

The projection kernel can update the particle position as well as its sampled
properties. A custom kernel is supplied through
`project_on_tracer_properties_kernel`; `projection_kernel_arguments` adds
solver values such as a cell time-step size when required.

The tracing kernels update only `Local` records and use `MoveState` to prevent
a second update during the same rank traversal. The particle sorting action
sets then restore the vertex and level association. @ref
page_peano4_particles_mesh_consistency describes the required follow-up
sweeps, rank-boundary transfer, and sieve redistribution.

# Parallel behaviour

Tracer particle containers make particle-bearing traversal steps serial within
each rank. MPI rank-boundary exchange maintains local and virtual copies with
one sweep of lag. The solver's non-particle steps can still use the ordinary
subtree-task decomposition.

The generic boundary exchange aliases wrapped periodic vertices but does not
mirror particle coordinates. The sieve applies periodic wrapping to particles
that it redistributes globally.

Trajectory files remain rank-local even after particles migrate. The stable
particle number is the key for joining their records.

*/
